actors are looking to renegotiate their  contracts in big budget movies scarlett  johansson the star of disney's latest  marvel movie black widow has filed a  lawsuit today in los angeles superior  court against disney traditional movie  contracts for a-list talent include  sizable upfront payments and a lucrative  back-end deal based mostly on box office  ticket sales but with more films going  straight to streaming platforms actors  are now looking for a piece of the  streaming profits the movie wouldn't  exist without actors and especially  actors of certain stature and with  certain names they drive people to those  theaters or they drive people to  download that subscription service and i  do believe that they should get a  portion of that paying stars a bigger  piece of digital sales would upend the  traditional pay structure for talent and  for some it could lead to tens of  millions of dollars in residual pay  money that agents and actors say is now  being left on the table here's a look at  how the scarlett johansson lawsuit  against disney could be a catalyst that  changes the way hollywood stars get paid  to understand what's at stake with  actors you have to understand how their  pay is tied to a film's distribution  cycle there are so many unique and  creative ways for actors and performers  to make additional compensation for  their participation in films natasha  matalana who's been representing actors  for over 15 years says depending on the  contract back-end bonuses in the long  run can be more lucrative for actors  than an upfront payment some of that  compensation is creatively carved out by  way of calling it a production fee or a  marketing fee or back end points those  bonuses are tied to how well the film  does at each point of the distribution  cycle traditionally that cycle looks  like this feature films start their  journey with a theatrical release that  can last anywhere from a few weeks to a  few months depending on how well it does  in the box office at this stage talent  contracts for actors can include a cut  of the opening box office sales after  it's run in the theaters a film would  then make its way onto dvd or  pay-per-view depending on their contract  an actor might make a cut of that as  well about six months after that the  title would become available on  streaming services like disney plus or  hulu this gives talent another  opportunity to cash in this time for  licensing deals with various streaming  content providers eventually if a film  was well received it could also end up  syndicated on television syndicated  films can continue to make money for  studios and talent as long as the  broadcaster continues to license and air  the film so that's what it looked like  before here's what it's starting to look  like today studios like disney and  warner media are releasing films both in  theaters for cinema goers and online for  the at-home audience at the same time  viewers who watch in theaters pay the  normal price per ticket for admission  and those who watch at home might pay  twice once for a monthly subscription  and depending on the streaming service  again with a surcharge for early access  to the film unlike box office retail dvd  and licensing streaming surcharges and  subscription fees are usually not split  with the talent depending on the actors  contract they may still retain their  backend residuals for things like dvd  sales foreign sales and pay-per-views  but those have always been far less  lucrative compared to box office bonuses  with direct-to-streaming studios have  found a promising solution for poor box  office sales actors and agents argue  that same-day streaming is in direct  competition with a theatrical release  and they're taking a huge loss in back  end residuals  well disney says they made about 60  million dollars just from the digital  side of that when they release black  widow dan gallagher who's been following  the rise in popularity of streaming  